<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donatello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1386–<a href="/years/1466">1466</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Donatello (c. 1386–1466) was a Florentine sculptor of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. Trained in Lorenzo Ghiberti's workshop, he worked in marble, bronze and wood, and his bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, the first free-standing male nude since antiquity, and his equestrian monument to Gattamelata in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> revived classical forms. His naturalism and expressive figures, such as the Penitent Magdalene, influenced generations of artists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donatello.jpg" alt="Donatello" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Bini · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donatello,_miracolo_della_mula,_1446-49_ca._(pd,_museo_antoniano)_37_uomo_che_si_allontana,_forse_autoritratto_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Donato di Niccolò di Betto Bardi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, probably in 1386, judging from his own tax declaration of July 1427, in which he claimed to be 41. His father, Niccolò di Betto Bardi, was a wool-stretcher and a member of the wool workers' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>. Donatello's first appearance in the records is in January 1401, when, aged about 15, he was accused in Pistoia of hitting a German with a stick. He appears to have befriended, and perhaps worked with, the goldsmith <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi">Filippo Brunelleschi</a>, some ten years older. All accounts describe him as amiable and well liked but poor at the business side of his career, accepting more commissions than he could handle, and he enjoyed steady Medici patronage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By early 1408 Donatello had the commission for a life-size prophet for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a>, to pair with one by Nanni di Banco; it was not placed as intended and appears to be lost. He also made statues for the niches of Orsanmichele, and developed stiacciato, a very shallow relief, with Saint George Freeing the Princess as an early milestone. Around 1425 he formed a partnership with the architect Michelozzo, which lasted nine years and ended partly because of Donatello's delays on an exterior pulpit for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prato-cathedral">Prato Cathedral</a>. In the 1430s and 1440s he made many dancing children, putti and spiritelli.</p><p>His bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, now in the Bargello, is his best-known work: the first known free-standing nude statue since antiquity, conceived fully in the round at nearly life size. It was probably commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a> between 1434 and 1440. In 1443 he was called to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> by the heirs of the condottiere Gattamelata, whose equestrian monument, cast mostly in 1447 or 1448 and finished in 1450, was installed in 1453. His ten years there are thought to have stimulated Padua's later table bronzes. He worked in Siena between 1457 and 1461, though ageing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Donatello remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> after returning from Siena and died in 1466, on 13 December. In his last years, with assistants, he made the reliefs that now form two pulpits in San Lorenzo, assembled in that form only in the following century. His influence can be seen in all Florentine sculpture of the 15th century and well beyond, and most of his major church works remain in place, with the main museum collection in the Bargello. Giorgio Vasari, writing around a century after the events, recognised his stature but made clear errors, placing his birth in 1403.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Donatello” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…century Filippo Brunelleschi’s dome of Florence Cathedral, completed in 1436, his use of linear perspective, the sculpture of <b>Donatello</b> and the painting of Masaccio launched the artistic Renaissance, followed by Sandro Botticelli, Leonardo da Vinci and Michelangelo, whose David was set up outside the Palazzo Vecchio in 1504.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1438" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Donatello"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5691 3800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5691 3800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1438">What was here in 1438? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
